
The data computed by this mandelbrot code mess may be dumped
out into a file within the users TMPDIR. If the user does not
have the environment variable TMPDIR set then there is no way
to output the data and the DUMPER button will not work.

The filename will always be the current time expressed in the
GMT0 timezone as YYYYMMDDHHMMSS such as 20220724124542. There
is no file extension as that is a wonky DOS concept.

The mandelbrot data displayed on the X11 screen is arranged in
a somewhat strange format. We see a grid of boxes on the screen
and they are all the same size. See the header file mandelbrot.h
where it says :

    /* How many little sample boxes are there within the plot
     * region?  We start with a 16x16 grid of these vbox
     * square sample sets. */
    #define VBOX_REAL_COUNT 16
    #define VBOX_IMAG_COUNT 16

Therefore, at the moment there are 256 of these little boxes on
the screen and they are arranged in a perfectly square which is
sixteen wide and sixteen high. This allows the the location of
a particular box to be addressed as a single hex byte 0xRJ where
the value R identifies the location across the bottom of the
screen from left to right and we begin counting at zero. The same
sort of numbering is used vertically with zero at the bottom and
fifteen at the top. Thus any given visual computation region may
easily be described in a single byte such as 0x3C for the visual
box at ( 3, 12 ). Note that the lower left box is ( 0, 0 ). These
are refered to a "vbox" regions on the screen and data is always
processed in terms of a "vbox" collection of samples.

Each of these "vbox" regions will contain a sample grid which is
also a square array and the defaults are described in the header
file mandelbrot.h thus :

    /* How many samples are there within a given vbox? */
    #define VBOX_SAMPLE_REAL 64
    #define VBOX_SAMPLE_IMAG 64

We may easily increase the sample grid within a vbox upwards with
the only limit being system memory. At the moment the code is a
bit messy in that all of the vbox data is kept in memory on the
heap regardless if we need it or not. At any given moment we may
have a display of data on the screen but the actual computation
stage only ever deals with a single sample within a single vbox
at any given moment. This may improve in the future. Maybe.

A quick calculation shows that we have 64 x 64 = 4096 samples in
any given vbox region. There are 16 x 16 = 256 of these vbox areas
and thus a total 256 x 4096 = 1048576 samples in the entire set.

Data may be dumped out into a binary file within the users TMPDIR
path with a header section and a data pack section. The header has
the following format :

    HEADER :

               4 byte uint32_t     num_elements
               4 byte uint32_t     mand_bail
               8 byte double       magnify
               8 byte double       real_translate
               8 byte double       imag_translate

Consider a sample dataset described with the input parameters :

    32768 268435456 0.399750960350502282381 0.205251797480741515756 4

Those input parameters refer to a central point on the complex plane
at ( 0.399750960350502282381, 0.205251797480741515756 ) and the entire
region we see computed on the screen will be magnified by 268435456.
The default region we see on the plot region is 4 units square with an
input magnify value of 1. Therefore the data region for the above input
will be 4 / 268435456 square. Thus all samples within all the vbox
regions must lay in a complex plane set about 1.490116x10^(-8) square.

The number 32768 will indicate the maximaum number of loops we may do
inside the mandelbrot computation before a "bail out" occurs. A loop
count less than 32768 will be reported as a trivial uint32_t integer
and the maximum value is simply considered zero. Therefore a maximum
loop count is usually displayed as a black point and this is thought
to be within the mandelbrot set. In actual fact the computation is an
infinite series but computers are terrible as that sort of thing. We
just give up after 32768 loops and consider the sample point to be a
zero value. All other values may be given some colour value in any way
that you please. Endless variations exist. The data dump files do not
report colour in any way whatsoever.

The complex plane point described above is treated as a translation of
the square sample set matrix away from the origin at ( 0, 0 ).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
HEADER Region :

Therefore the header data for the above parameters will be :

  Datatype        Variable_Name   Hexadecimal           Decimal
  4 byte uint32_t num_elements            0x00100000h =   1048576
  4 byte uint32_t mand_bail               0x00008000h =     32768
  8 byte double   magnify         0x41b0000000000000h = 268435456
  8 byte double   real_translate  0x3fd995850d500000h = 0.399750960...
  8 byte double   imag_translate  0x3fca45b0ded00000h = 0.205251797...

A big endian machine would represent the data exactly as we see above
whereas a little endian machine would output :

         00 00 10 00 00 80 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 b0 41
         00 00 50 0d 85 95 d9 3f 00 00 d0 de b0 45 ca 3f

Given that little endian machines are vastly more popular than the big
endian variations the code always outputs in little endian format. This
makes for a bit of overhead on systems such as IBM Power4 or POWER8 in
big endian mode. That same is true for Fujitsu/Oracle SPARC64 servers.
The process of swapping around bytes on those systems is trivial and we
will always get an output data dump file in little endian format. All of
the ARM systems ( 64-bit and 32-bit ) as well as x86 systems seem to be
little endian only. The RISC-V rv64imafdc system tested was based on the
SiFive UnMatched prototype board which has a U740 SoC chip processor and
the data format in memory is little endian. It should be noted that the
IEEE754-2008 data type formats are consistent across all machines tested
where the only variation is seen with long double 128-bit data. It was
seen that all x86 systems fail to implement such a datatype. In fact
the x86 systems will use a full 16bytes of memory to represent a 128bit
data element but only ten bytes contain data in some 80bit proprietary
Intel format. The remaining six bytes are wasted memory and this x86
systems literally throw away 32.5% of the memory for long double data.
One must resort to software implementations. Most IBM systems seemed
to use a 128-bit datatype which is actually two 64-bit double elements in
memory and therefore not actually the IEEE754-2008 compliant format. An
IBM POWER9 server does implement actual 128-bit math operations in hardware
however this sort of compliance with the floating point specifications in
hardware is very rare and was only ever seen on POWER9 and an IBM Mainframe
class systems. Thus all computations are done with 64-bit datatypes and
this allows for excellent comparison across a multitude of systems. We
should note that most NVidia GPU hardware seen were able to perform
the 64bit computations but at a vastly reduced performance cost. Only
the float type 32bit operations seem to perform well. This is likely
the result of a factory option that disables 64bit operations on the
NVidia hardware and we only see reasonable performance on "Quadro"
type GPU options such as the NVidia GV100 and similar.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
DATAPACK Region :

The data for all num_elements is arranged in vbox order. This may seem
to be counter intuitive however the objective was to arrange for small
sections of computation that may be distributed across POSIX threads
or multiple network attached systems. Thus the vbox data sample sets
are considered to be the unit of work for this process. The data pack
region of the data dump is arranged as real axis samples from left to
right within a given vbox. Once a single horizontal row of samples has
been written the next row will be upwards in a positive direction on
the imaginary axis and still inside a single vbox. Thus a single vbox
will consist of ( VBOX_SAMPLE_REAL ) x ( VBOX_SAMPLE_IMAG ) samples.
The entire data pack region of the dump file will have a total number
of ( VBOX_REAL_COUNT ) x ( VBOX_IMAG_COUNT ) such records. The first
vbox region dumped with be ( 0, 0 ) with the next being ( 1, 0 ) and
onwards across the vbox row from left to right. The next row of vbox
regions is then dumped on the row upwards along the complex plane.

The exact order of vbox regions dumped is thus :

    ( 0, 0), ( 1, 0), ( 2, 0), ( 3, 0), .... (13, 0), (14, 0), (15, 0),
    ( 0, 1), ( 1, 1), ( 2, 1), ( 3, 1), .... (13, 1), (14, 1), (15, 1),
    ( 0, 2), ( 1, 2), ( 2, 2), ( 3, 2), .... (13, 2), (14, 2), (15, 2),
         .
         .                .... ( 3,12), ....
         .
    ( 0,14), ( 1,14), ( 2,14), ( 3,14), .... (13,14), (14,14), (15,14),
    ( 0,15), ( 1,15), ( 2,15), ( 3,15), .... (13,15), (14,15), (15,15)

That order above covers all of the current 256 vbox regions from the
lowermost row and upwards to the top row as seen on screen. Each of
these vbox regions will contain 4096 samples in a similar order to
cover the VBOX_SAMPLE_REAL x VBOX_SAMPLE_IMAG total. Thus the actual
sample data [ r, j] within any given vbox such as ( 3, 12 )  will be
is ordered thus : 

    [ 0, 0], [ 1, 0], [ 2, 0], [ 3, 0], .... [61, 0], [62, 0], [63, 0],
    [ 0, 1], [ 1, 1], [ 2, 1], [ 3, 1], .... [61, 1], [62, 1], [63, 1],
         .
         .
         .
    [ 0,62], [ 1,62], [ 2,62], [ 3,62], .... [61,62], [62,62], [63,62],
    [ 0,63], [ 1,63], [ 2,63], [ 3,63], .... [61,63], [62,63], [63,63]

This order will cover all 4096 samples within any given vbox.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SAMPLE Record:

Each sample consists of the follow data :

               8 byte double       real_coord
               8 byte double       imag_coord
               4 byte uint32_t     mandel_val

Thus we have 20 bytes per sample and a very typical output will look
like so : 

         57 0e 00 00 00 00 4e 06 85 95 d9 3f 00 00 e4 ce b0 45 ca 3f

If we report that sample record in a more natural big endian format
we see : 

  Datatype        Variable_Name   Hexadecimal           Decimal
  4 byte uint32_t mandel_val              0x00000e57h = 3671
  8 byte double   real_coord      0x3fd99585064e0000h = 0.3997509538..
  8 byte double   imag_coord      0x3fca45b0cee40000h = 0.2052517900..

The actual value of the 64bit floating point data may be :

              real_coord = +3.9975095382396830245852e-01       
              imag_coord = +2.0525179006654070690274e-01      

I would question the precision of the above given that floating point
data usually can only estimate some actual decimal value. A number as
trivial as 0.1 decimal does not exist within floating point. Thus the
above values are best estimate for the complex plane sample point for
our 64bit limitations.

The question would be how to read in all of the data dump file such
that the data is well ordered into data structures that represent all
of the above. It seems one may do a trivial loop set thus : 

  /* process the initial header data first */
  /* outer most vbox loop is for vertical upwards count on
   * the imaginary axis */
  for ( Vj = 0; Vj < VBOX_IMAG_COUNT; Vj++ ) {
      /* internal vbox loop is for left to right count on
       * the real axis */
      for ( Vr = 0; Vr < VBOX_REAL_COUNT; Vr++ ) {
          /* process samples in a similar order */
          for ( Sj = 0; Sj < VBOX_SAMPLE_IMAG; Sj++ ) {
              for ( Sr = 0; Sr < VBOX_SAMPLE_REAL; Sr++ ) {

                  /* read 32bit uint32_t   mandel_val */
                  /* read 64bit double     real_coord */
                  /* read 64bit double     imag_coord */

                  /* use array_offset(Vr, Vj, Sr, Sj) to save the data */


The above should do the job nicely. However there is the possibility
that the machine is big endian and in such a situation we need to swap
the order of the data bytes. It is best to read the 64bit double data
as if it were uint64_t type and then reverse those bytes. This will
avoid the possibility that a little endian floating point double data
element would be interpreted as a NaN or sub-normal as well as the
problem of floating point exception flags.

Another possible situation is that we may wish to seek into the data
dump file and read a particular record or even an entire vbox. The
process should be very similar to the array_offset computation for
our data in memory.



